// Lesson 3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns & Time
// Adds rate limiting to the turret the student built in 3.1 and 3.2. They left
// 3.2 firing once per FRAME, because the range check that calls Fire() runs
// inside Update() — a condition, re-evaluated every frame, not an event.
//
// Scaffolding: GUIDED (level 2). The shape of the file is given — the student
// has written Update() before, twice — and the two lines carrying the new idea
// are gaps: the gate condition and the line that stamps the new moment. Those
// two are the whole mechanism; everything around them is 3.1 and 1.3.
//
// Four timing approaches are laid out for comparison (Time.time timestamp,
// countdown decremented by Time.deltaTime, InvokeRepeating, a coroutine loop);
// the student chooses and justifies one. The walkthrough builds the reference
// approach — the Time.time timestamp, the quick reference's "Cooldown" pattern
// row and the field the Unit 3 docs call lastAttackTime.
//
// FACTUAL GROUND TRUTH, do not "fix" these:
//   - Time.deltaTime IS correct inside a countdown timer. This lesson's rule
//     that velocity code takes no deltaTime is about Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity,
//     which is already measured per second. The two rules do not conflict, and
//     the table prices both rows without ranking them.
//   - no "Attack Cooldown" field exists anywhere in the project — every knob in
//     this lesson is one the student adds themselves.
//
// STRUCTURE (this pass, "teach it once"): ONE editor block, one table, the
// walkthrough, one spec+Socratic block. The explanation of WHY the turret hosed
// bullets lives in the walkthrough, right beside the line that fixes it, so the
// three places that used to explain it again are gone: the lesson-lead's
// restatement, the editor step that explained the frame rate, and the Console
// step that repeated the missing-stamp failure the walkthrough already names.
// The table's cost column now prices each row in one clause instead of a
// paragraph — it is a lookup, and it was carrying the walkthrough's teaching.
// "Update(" is deliberately absent from the editor half and the Break It Down
// pointers, so the term appears in the two sections where it is load-bearing.
//
// Answer-box ids are positional and persisted, so the socratic block keeps its
// three questions in order, and the decompose block keeps both of its turns
// (dc1:q1, dc1:q2) and its one gap.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the cooldown. Level 2, so exactly
// one part is withheld — the one with no precedent in this course. Everything
// this project has remembered so far (a coin count, health, whether a door is
// open) has been a number that changes; today's memory is a MOMENT, which is
// only meaningful next to the clock it came from. Its reading stays attached,
// because finding the answer in the docs is the skill being practised.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'The turret should shoot at me — but not sixty times a second. "Shoot at me, sometimes" ' +
    'is the whole feature, and there is nothing in it yet that a computer can act on.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Notice the player is still inside the radius',
      does: 'The distance test from 3.1, re-evaluated every frame: true for sixty frames in a row, which is what a condition is.',
      doc: 'the range check in your own 3.1 script, and the `Vector3.Distance(a, b)` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '3.1.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Remember when the last shot happened',
      doc: 'the `Time.time` row in the quick reference, and the deadline you wrote in 1.3.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
      blank: true,
    },
    {
      name: 'Refuse to fire until enough time has passed',
      does: 'One condition attached to the range check with `&&`: the clock, against that remembered moment plus the gap you want.',
      doc: 'the **Cooldown** row of the pattern table in the quick reference, and the gate you wrote in 1.3.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Record the new moment the instant a shot leaves',
      does: 'One line, run only when the turret actually fires — the only thing that closes the gate again.',
      doc: 'the **Cooldown** row in the pattern table (`act, set last = Time.time`).',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Three of the four parts are code you have already written. The one with no precedent in this ' +
    'course is the memory: a field that holds a moment rather than a count.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, in one search? "Fire less often" cannot be ' +
    'looked up. "Compare a timestamp against the current time" can.',
  turns: [
    'The table in the code half prices four ways to time this action. Which did you choose, and what does your choice cost you — in your own words, not the table\'s?',
    'Today\'s memory holds a moment on the clock. If the turret had to charge for a second before each shot, what would that field have to hold instead — and which of the four approaches would you reach for?',
  ],
};

// Level 2: the frame of the file is given and the two lines carrying the new
// idea are gaps. Every block opens with a comment saying where in the file it
// goes — the knob with the other fields, the seed inside Start(), the gate on
// the 3.1 range check inside Update(). The `why` on each line is where this
// lesson's mechanism is explained, and it is explained nowhere else: the table,
// the spec and the checklist all point here rather than repeating it.
const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I stop shooting too fast?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/TurretController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro: 'Your 3.1 and 3.2 turret does not change: it still finds the target, measures the distance and spawns a projectile at the muzzle. Today adds one knob, one field and one condition. Keep your own names and read the shape.',
  level: 2,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Declare the knob and the memory',
      lead: 'Both lines go in the class body, outside every method — the placement rule from 1.3.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — class body, above Start(), with your other fields.', what: '' },
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float cooldown = 1.5f;', what: 'The knob: how many seconds must pass between shots. `[SerializeField]` puts it in the Inspector, so a designer sets the pacing without opening your code.' , why: '**It has to be a `float`.** An `int` rounds every Inspector value down to a whole second, so `0.5` quietly becomes `0` — back to a turret that fires every frame, from a type the Inspector never shows you.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    private float lastAttackTime;', what: '**The memory:** one float holding the moment, on the game clock, when this turret last fired. `private`, because it is the turret\'s own bookkeeping.' , why: '**Where this line lives is the memory.** Move it inside the method that reads it and it is rebuilt from nothing every frame, holding the same empty value each time. That is the scope rule from 1.3: the same line in the wrong place is not a memory, it is a habit.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Seed the memory before the first frame',
      lead: 'Once, before any frame has run.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — inside Start(), with your 3.1 target lookup.', what: '' },
        { code: '        lastAttackTime = -cooldown;', what: 'A first value, so the turret may fire the moment the player walks into range instead of serving a penalty for a shot it never took.' , why: '**Zero is a real moment on this clock.** Left at its default, the field claims the last shot happened when the scene began, and the gate below would refuse to fire for the first `cooldown` seconds with the player standing in front of it.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Close the gate in Update()',
      lead: 'The range check stays as you wrote it in 3.1. It is just no longer the only thing deciding whether the turret fires.',
      lines: [
        { code: '// TurretController.cs — inside Update(), on the range check from 3.1.', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (distance <= range && ______________________)', blank: true, what: 'The gate. Keep the range half, add `&&`, and add the second condition: fire only when enough time has passed since the last shot.' , hint: 'The clock that only counts up is the `Time.time` row in the quick reference; the last shot is the field from step 1; the gap is the knob. Put those three into one comparison — and ask whether `==` could ever be true.' , why: '**`==` is the wrong operator.** Frames do not land on exact times, so the clock almost never *equals* your remembered moment plus the cooldown — it jumps past it. `>=` means *at or past*, which is what "enough time has passed" says. The `&&` is not decoration: drop the range half and the turret fires at nothing, on cadence, from wherever it stands.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            Fire();', what: 'Your firing method from 3.2 — the one that spawns a projectile at the muzzle. Unchanged, however you named it.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '            ________________', blank: true, what: 'The line that closes the gate again. It runs only when a shot has actually left the barrel: write the current moment into the field that remembers the last one.' , hint: 'The field remembers a moment, and the gate compared it against the clock you are standing on right now. What is that clock\'s current value?' , why: '**The most common bug in this lesson.** Leave it out and the gate never closes: the field keeps its old value, the condition stays true on the next frame and every frame after, and the turret is back to firing at your frame rate — the bug you started with, rebuilt inside the fix. Stamping it inside `Fire()` instead protects any other caller of that method; keep it here for now, where the gate and its consequence sit on the same three lines.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class TurretController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float range = 6f;              // your number from 3.1
    [SerializeField] private float cooldown = 1.5f;         // the new knob
    [SerializeField] private Transform playerTransform;     // however you acquired it in 3.1
    [SerializeField] private GameObject projectilePrefab;
    [SerializeField] private Transform spawnPoint;

    private float lastAttackTime;                           // the memory: a MOMENT, not a count

    void Start()
    {
        // ...the target lookup you chose in 3.1 stays here...

        // One cooldown BEFORE the game started, so the first shot is legal
        // the moment the player walks into range.
        lastAttackTime = -cooldown;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (playerTransform == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        float distance = Vector3.Distance(transform.position, playerTransform.position);

        // Both halves must be true: in range, AND enough time since the last shot.
        if (distance <= range && Time.time >= lastAttackTime + cooldown)
        {
            Fire();

            lastAttackTime = Time.time;                     // closes the gate again
        }
    }

    private void Fire()
    {
        // ...the projectile spawn you wrote in 3.2, unchanged...
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it: your names, your range, your cooldown default and your 3.2 spawn are yours, and the shape is what has to match.',
};

const body = `<h1>3.3 — Fire Rate: Cooldowns &amp; Time</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Last lesson your turret did not fire shots, it fired a <em>stream</em> — every frame, for as long
    as you stood in range. Today it learns to wait.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I stop shooting too fast?', 2)}
${renderDecompose('3.3', DECOMPOSE, 2)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> the two <strong>Turret</strong> instances in the
            <strong>Overworld</strong>, then <strong>Inspector → Turret Controller:</strong> the two
            places today's knob will appear. Instance values override the prefab's, so the two
            turrets are allowed to disagree on purpose.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view → press Play</strong> and stand inside one turret's radius for five
            seconds, then <strong>Console →</strong> count the lines that appear. Write the number
            down: it is the rate your frames are running at, not a decision anybody made.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the walkthrough is done — Inspector → Turret Controller → your cooldown
            knob</strong> (it is there now that the field exists). Set one turret to <code>2</code>
            and the other to <code>0.5</code>, then <strong>Game view → press Play</strong>, stand
            inside a radius, and time ten shots with a stopwatch. Then change the value <em>while the
            game is running</em> and watch the pacing change with it.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Pick a Timing Model</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~35 min</span>
    </div>

    <table>
        <tr>
            <th>Approach</th>
            <th>What you store</th>
            <th>The gate</th>
            <th>What it costs you</th>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(a) A <code>Time.time</code> timestamp</strong></td>
            <td>One float: the moment the last shot happened.</td>
            <td><code>Time.time &gt;= lastShot + cooldown</code></td>
            <td>One line of state, and one failure mode you will meet in the walkthrough. It
                remembers <strong>when</strong> the last shot happened, not <strong>how many</strong>
                are owed — a second press inside the window is discarded, not queued.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(b) A countdown timer</strong></td>
            <td>One float: seconds <em>remaining</em> until the next shot is allowed.</td>
            <td><code>timer &lt;= 0f</code>, then <code>timer = cooldown</code></td>
            <td>Something has to tick it down, every frame, including the frames where the turret is
                not firing. In exchange it reads as "seconds until I can shoot", which is what a
                designer wants to see.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(c) <code>InvokeRepeating</code></strong></td>
            <td>Nothing at all — the engine holds the schedule.</td>
            <td><code>InvokeRepeating(nameof(Fire), 0f, cooldown)</code></td>
            <td>Almost no code, and that is the trap: your method is called on cadence whether or not
                the player is in range, so the range check has to move inside it. Cancelling is by
                method <em>name</em>, so a rename breaks the cancel silently.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>(d) A coroutine loop</strong></td>
            <td>A running coroutine handle.</td>
            <td><code>while (true) { yield return new WaitForSeconds(t); }</code></td>
            <td>A <code>StartCoroutine</code> call, a stored handle to stop it, and a second mental
                model of when your code runs. It is the only row that expresses "charge, then burst,
                then rest" cleanly.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Your Row</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification — for whichever row you chose.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>The knob.</strong> Which serialized float lets a designer set the gap between shots without recompiling, and what default will you ship for it?</li>
            <li><strong>The memory.</strong> What does <em>your</em> row keep between frames — a moment, a duration, a handle, or nothing the engine is not already holding — and why can it not live inside the method that reads it?</li>
            <li><strong>The gate and the reset.</strong> Which two conditions must <em>both</em> be true before the turret may fire, and which operator says "both"? What changes at the instant a shot actually leaves?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Choosing a Timing Model</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Trace your own 3.2 build: standing inside the radius for one second, how many projectile clones did your firing method create — and why did that number depend on the machine rather than on you?</li>
<li>Why can't you call a "wait one second" function inside <code>Update()</code>? Describe what the player would see on screen during that wait, and explain why a game engine refuses to let you block the frame.</li>
<li>Row (a) stores a <em>moment</em>; row (b) stores a <em>duration</em>. Which one can you print in the Console and have a player immediately understand, and which one is easier to compare against a designer's tuning value?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

